<h2>Things I Care About</h2>
<p>
  I am a big fan of rationalism and <a href="/effective-altruism"
    >effective altruism</a
  >. I think at some point this century, the Singularity will occur, in which
  AIs become smarter than people in the non-metaphorical impossible-to-miss way
  that results in things like planets being dismantled, and that we are
  currently not on track for this to happen in a way that leaves survivors.
</p>
<p>
  The most effective way for an individual donor to give is through a <a
    href="https://www.givingwhatwecan.org/donor-lottery"
  >
    donor lottery</a
  >. Giving What We Can has, historically, run one once a year, though they
  didn't run one in early 2026. The most important object-level issue, for both
  effort and money to be allocated to, is <a
    href="https://nitter.net/ESYudkowsky/status/1570967796582076416"
  >
    AI notkilleveryoneism</a
  >. The situation here is currently looking dire; <strong>
    please read <a
      href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/uMQ3cqWDPHhjtiesc/agi-ruin-a-list-of-lethalities"
    >
      AGI Ruin: A List of Lethalities</a
    ></strong
  > for a view of how difficult this issue is, and how completely unprepared anyone
  is to deal with it, or <a href="https://intelligence.org/the-problem/"
    >The Problem</a
  > for a more polished overview that came later with a greater focus on the current
  best-looking strategy of setting up an international agreement in order to prevent
  the creation of smarter-than-human AI.
</p>
<p>
  Beyond AI notkilleveryoneism, I see effective altruism as being a group of
  people who share the common interest of making the world better by allocating
  their time and money towards where it can be best used to that end;
  notkilleveryoneism, global poverty and animal welfare seem like an eclectic
  mix, but, ideally, our hearts are in the same place.
</p>

<p>
  In that spirit, I also want to see malaria gone. <a
    href="https://ourworldindata.org/malaria"
    >Our World In Data's page about Malaria</a
  > gives an overview of the current state of play of malaria (though I haven't found
  the ultimate malaria primer; let me know if you do). Giving What We Can is currently
  hosting a <a href="https://www.givingwhatwecan.org/malaria">Malaria Fund</a> which
  grants onwards to the <a href="https://www.givewell.org/charities/amf"
    >Against Malaria Foundation</a
  > (which funds mass bednet distributions - e.g. <a
    href="https://www.againstmalaria.com/Distribution1.aspx?ProposalID=296"
    >this one</a
  > to Literally Everyone in South Sudan) and the <a
    href="https://www.givewell.org/charities/malaria-consortium"
    >Malaria Consortium</a
  > (which gives out <a
    href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240073692"
    >sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine plus amodiaquine</a
  > once a month during the high-malaria months), both chosen as the top malaria charities
  that Givewell thinks it can prove are that good.
</p>
